roofline variants `fused_c4` / `fused_c4_layer` of workloads/clusterfusion_llama2_7b_decoder.yaml and `fused_c4` of
workloads/clusterfusion_deepseek_v2_lite_mla.yaml (one worktree, roofline/build/src/ClusterFusion__clusterfusion__
residual_out_c4_fix/, applied by the runner, never in third_party/). Combined diff against the pin = the fixes below.
Summary: 1 residual_out (Llama; GPU-confirmed race); 2 TMA-buffer WAR (Llama: GPU-confirmed race and fix; DeepSeek:
same code pattern, not observed; plus two DeepSeek local_qkv WARs of the same class); 3 dsm.cuh cluster_reduce
hardening (spec-required, not observed).

1. (= patches/clusterfusion__residual_out.patch, unchanged) Cross-cluster race on `residual` in LlamaDecoderLayerKernel
   (include/H100/llama/kernel_sglang.cuh): every one of the 32 head clusters reads input + residual and wrote the sum
   back into `residual`, so a CTA reading after another cluster's write normalised 2*input + residual (wrong q/k/v
   for a whole head; confirmed on GPU 3 in R4 at cluster 4 and cluster 1). The kernel gets a `half* residual_output`
   parameter and writes input + residual there; `residual` is only read. The dispatcher allocates residual_output =
   torch::empty_like(residual) (no kernel) and returns it in place of `residual`; the Python signature is unchanged.
   Precedent: upstream's include/H100/llama/kernel_batch_sglang.cuh.

2. (TMA double-buffer WAR, kernel_sglang.cuh: the Q, K and V GEMV loops and the flash-decoding loop.) In each loop
   iteration `id`, tid 0 first issues the TMA load of tile id into buffer id % 2 and only then waits for tile id - 1;
   buffer id % 2 still holds tile id - 2, which the other warps read in iteration id - 1. Nothing orders those reads
   before the refill: the cuda::barrier of the buffer only counts the arrivals made *before* the reads, so tid 0
   (warp 0) can be one iteration ahead of warps 1-3 and overwrite the tile they are still reading (the O-projection
   loop already has a block.sync at the end of every iteration and is not affected). Fix: block.sync() at the top of
   these four loops, before the refill. Evidence (GPU 3, 1635 MHz, 200 launches on identical inputs at S = 1k,
   roofline/build/scratch_c4fix2/diag.py, pin + fix 1 + fix 3): 34/200 launches had a deviating k_new or v_new, always
   in one head of heads {6, 7, 30, 31} only, and always in whole 32-row blocks 32-63, 64-95 or 96-127 (= the GEMV
   rows of warps 1, 2, 3; never rows 0-31 = warp 0, which contains tid 0), errors up to 0.65. With this fix and fix 1
   only: 0/600 deviating launches (S = 1k, 4k, 16k); with fixes 1-3: 0/600, and all 600 within the manifest tolerances
   against the unfused sequence. Why only at cluster 4: the dynamic shared memory is ~101 KB, so two CTAs can share an
   SM; with 128 CTAs in clusters that must fit in one GPC, some clusters (the four affected heads) are packed two CTAs
   per SM and their warps drift apart; the cluster-1 build (32 CTAs, one per SM) has the same loops but did not show
   the race. Cost: kernel median +0.1 % (S 1k) to +1.1 % (S 16k).
   DeepSeek kernel (include/H100/deepseek/kernel.cuh, compiled into the same .so): the same refill-before-wait loops
   (W_q, W_q_pe, W_kv and its per-slice restart, W_k_pe, flash-decoding over the latent cache; W_uv for parity with
   the cluster-1 build, where it has 8 iterations) get the same block.sync(). Not observed there (the pin and the
   earlier patch passed 20/20); added because the code pattern is identical. Not changed: the W_uk loop (one
   iteration, preceded by a block.sync) and the W_o loop (block.sync at the end of every iteration).
   Also in the DeepSeek kernel, same class (shared-memory WAR without a barrier), code reading only: the W_UK result
   (pin l.386, local_qkv[cluster_block_id * 128 + row]) and the W_UV partial (pin l.635, local_qkv[row]) are written
   into local_qkv[0..128) by each warp as soon as it finishes, while in CTA 0 the other warps may still read that
   range as their input (q_nope, resp. a_lat slice 0). One block.sync() before each write (the cluster-1 patch keeps
   the W_UK results in registers behind a block.sync already, and gets the same W_UV line).

3. (Bulk-push AllReduce hardening, include/dsm.cuh, cluster_reduce, the non-QUK_DEEPSEEK branch used by Stage::LINEAR
   (q|k|v) and Stage::ATTN (attention output); only exists for CLUSTER_SIZE > 1. Required by the PTX memory model; NOT
   observed on the GPU: fix 2 alone removes every deviation seen.) Each round, tid 0 arrives with expect_tx on its own
   mbarrier, pushes `src` with cp.async.bulk.shared::cluster into the peer's `dst` (completing the *peer's* mbarrier),
   then all threads wait on their own mbarrier and immediately do src += dst. The own mbarrier only says that the
   copy *into* this CTA has landed; a shared::cluster bulk copy has no read-completion signal of its own, so nothing
   guarantees that this CTA's outgoing copy has finished reading src before src is overwritten (warp 1 can even pass
   the wait between tid 0's arrive and its copy issue). (a) one cluster.sync() after the wait, before the reduce-add:
   every outgoing copy is exactly one peer's incoming copy, so after it all copies of the round are complete. (b)
   fence.proxy.async.shared::cta before each barrier that precedes a bulk copy reading generic-written src (entry and
   end of each round) and fence.mbarrier_init.release.cluster after mbarrier.init (re-done on every call, targeted by
   the peers' complete_tx). Rounds, partners and sizes are unchanged. Cost: 4 extra cluster barriers per launch,
   kernel median about +0.7 to +1.5 % on top of fix 2. dsm.cuh is shared: the DeepSeek kernel in the same .so gets it
   too in this worktree (its QUK_DEEPSEEK all-gather branch, which never modifies src, is untouched).
